<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serampore College</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore">Serampore</a> College is a college in Serampore, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, founded in 1818 by the Baptist missionaries William Carey, Joshua Marshman and William Ward. In 1827 the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> granted it a charter to award degrees, and it still confers theology degrees on colleges across India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serampore-college.jpg" alt="Serampore College" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: gangulybiswarup · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serampore_College_-_Hooghly_2017-07-06_0840-0860.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Serampore Trio opened the college with 37 students. Before 1818 they had already taught their own children and Indian children, girls among them. Their aim was to teach arts and sciences to students of every &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, colour or country&quot; and to train people for ministry in the growing church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and their prospectus proposed &quot;A College for the instruction of Asiatic Christian and other Youth in Eastern Literature and European Science&quot;. No denominational test was applied to the teachers, so the college, ecumenical from the start, had no automatic support from any one church.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore">Serampore</a> was then a Danish colony. The royal charter of 23 February 1827, issued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> by King Frederick VI after Joshua Marshman visited him in August 1826, allowed degrees in arts and theology and named William Carey, Joshua Marshman and his son John Clark Marshman to the first council. It became the third Danish university, after Copenhagen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> sold its Indian possessions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1845, the college carried on under a master and council. The Baptist Missionary Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> took over its management in 1856, and in 1857 it became a constituent college of the new University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. In 1883 it closed as an arts college and became a Christian training institution and theological school for the Baptist churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>; affiliated again with Calcutta in 1911, it was authorised to award the Bachelor of Arts in 1913.</p><p>The first Bachelor of Divinity students graduated on 4 December 1915. In the centenary year, 1918, the Bengal Legislative Council passed the Serampore College Act, which enlarged the council and created an interdenominational senate to confer theological degrees for all Christian denominations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. By 1960 twenty other colleges and seminaries were affiliated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The college has a theological faculty and a separate college of arts, science and commerce, whose degrees are awarded by the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. The Senate administers the theological colleges affiliated to it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, Bangladesh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Its motto, from Proverbs 3:35 in the Vulgate, is Gloriam Sapientes Possidebunt, &quot;The wise shall possess glory&quot;. The Carey Library holds 16,000 rare volumes, and India issued a stamp depicting the college on 7 June 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Serampore College” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Serampore near Calcutta, acquired in 1755, became a centre of printing and of <b>Serampore College</b>, founded by English Baptists.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-india">Danish India</a></li></ul></section>
